    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f726a67d-81f9-5e52-b352-cf0e4487e7cb",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bd7b868b-5f2e-5467-a4ff-c0ce19494ff5",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
