    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3c8c05b4-e98e-5590-a35d-5ea753c58db3",
      ":question/problem": "If $f(x)={4}^{-5{x}^{3}+1}$, find ${f}^{\\prime}(x)$.\n\n${f}^{\\prime}(x)$$=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot {4}^{-5{x}^{3}+1}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={4}^{u},\\,u=-5{x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot (-15{x}^{2}) \\\\ & =(-15{x}^{2})\\cdot {4}^{u}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-15{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{-5{x}^{3}+1}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
